.
Usage: . FILE [ARGS]

Read FILE and run it in the CURRENT shell, rather than starting a new one.
Variables it sets, directories it cd's to, and functions it defines all
survive, because nothing was forked.

	. ./settings.sh		load settings into this shell
	sh ./settings.sh	run it in a child -- the changes are lost

That difference is the whole reason this exists. `source` is the bash
spelling; ash has only the dot.

This page is stored as "dot" because a file cannot be named "." -- that
name always means the directory itself. learn translates it for you, so
`learn ref .` works.

See also: sh, exec, eval
